 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Yes, it's getting harder, though the change is gradual.** Queensland's unemployment rate was 4.5% in September 2026 (seasonally adjusted). A year earlier, in September 2025, it was 4.2%. In January 2025 it was 4.0%.\n\n**Queensland**\n- Unemployment rate: 4.5% in both August and September 2026. That's the highest in the period I checked (January 2025 onward).\n- Number unemployed: about 144,000 in September 2026. That's up from about 131,000 a year earlier and 123,000 in January 2025.\n- Employment-to-population ratio: 63.9% in September 2026, down from 64.3% a year earlier and 64.6% in January 2025. A smaller share of the population is in work.\n- Participation: 66.9%, little changed from 67.1% a year earlier. People haven't been leaving the labour force, so the rise in unemployment is real.\n- Employment is still growing. It reached about 3.04 million in September 2026, up from about 3.01 million a year earlier. The labour force has grown faster than jobs, at about 3.19 million.\n\n**Compared with Australia**\n- The national unemployment rate was 4.7% in September 2026. That's up from 4.4% a year earlier and 4.1% in January 2025.\n- Queensland is still a bit below the national rate, but both have risen by roughly 0.3 to 0.6 points over the period.\n\n**Caveat:** Queensland's monthly figures are noisy. They have swung between 3.7% and 4.4% from month to month (for example, 3.7% in May 2026 and 4.3% in June). Single months can mislead. The more reliable signal is the steady climb since mid-2026, together with the falling employment-to-population ratio.\n\nJob seekers face more competition than a year or two ago. The labour market is still historically fairly tight.",
